Q: Is there a tidy way to narrow types as a result of Jest assertions? A: <p>I know in the question I was asking about jest, but it's worth mentioning that Vitest's <a href="https://vitest.dev/api/expect.html#expect-unreachable" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>expect.unreachable</code></a> does what I want here:</p>
<pre><code>describe(getData, () =&gt; {
  it(&quot;behaves correctly&quot;, () =&gt; {
    const result = getData();

    if (result.isLoading) {
      expect.unreachable(&quot;It shouldn't be loading&quot;); 
    }

    expect(result.data).toBe(&quot;hello world&quot;);
  });
});

</code></pre>
<p>You can achieve this by returning a function that has a return type of <code>never</code></p>
<p>Could just be a</p>
<pre><code>function unreachable(message: string): never {
    throw new Error(`
Test encountered an unreachable block with message: '${message}'
`);
}
</code></pre>

Q: React form with &lt;Popup&gt; alert message instead of alert() A: <p>Here's a way how you can exactly use a reactjs-popup:</p>

<pre><code>import React from "react";
import Popup from "reactjs-popup";

export default () =&gt; (
  &lt;Popup trigger={&lt;button&gt; Trigger&lt;/button&gt;} position="right center"&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;Popup content here !!&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/Popup&gt;
);
</code></pre>

<p>All you need to do is put a trigger for it and it'll work</p>

<p>For help: <a href="https://react-popup.netlify.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://react-popup.netlify.com/</a></p>
